---
import SiteLayout from '../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import ArticleFrame from '../../../components/ArticleFrame.astro';
const toc=[{id:'text-mask',label:'Create the text mask'},{id:'fit',label:'Move, resize, rotate'},{id:'batch',label:'Use it in a batch'},{id:'limitations',label:'Limitations'}];
const related=[{href:'/remove-watermark/',label:'Open Remove Watermark'},{href:'/guides/how-to-remove-watermark-from-image/',label:'Watermark removal guide'},{href:'/guides/how-to-remove-gemini-watermark-from-image/',label:'Gemini logo guide'}];
---
<SiteLayout title="How to Remove a Text Watermark from an Image | ConvertAnyFile" description="Use a solid manual text-shaped mask, auto-fit it to visible watermark characters, move, resize or rotate it, and repair selected pixels locally in your browser.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="site-container hero-inner"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/guides/">Guides</a><span>/</span><span>Text Watermark</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Image Editing Guide</span><h1>How to remove a visible text watermark</h1><p>Type a visible word, number, or phrase to create a solid text-shaped mask, then position it over the watermark before local repair.</p></div></section>
<ArticleFrame toc={toc} related={related} asideNote="The text field is a manual mask builder, not OCR or automatic text detection.">
<h2 id="text-mask">1. Choose Text and enter the visible watermark</h2><p>Open <a href="/remove-watermark/">Remove Watermark</a>, add an image, and choose <strong>Text</strong>. Enter the visible word, number, or short phrase. The editor creates a bold solid text-shaped mask instead of selecting a full rectangular block.</p><p>The selection bounds are fitted to the typed content. A short value such as a single digit stays compact, which makes it practical to move that mask close to an image corner. This is deliberately manual: the browser does not scan the image for words or decide which text should be removed.</p>
<h2 id="fit">2. Move, resize, and rotate the text-shaped mask</h2><p>Drag inside the selected mask to move it. Use a square corner handle to resize the footprint, or drag the round handle to rotate it over diagonal text. Keep the solid purple glyph mask over the complete visible character strokes you want repaired.</p><p>The mask uses a browser font as a selection aid, so it may not exactly reproduce a custom logo font. If the watermark includes a much thicker outline, glow, shadow, curved baseline, or decorative elements, add a Rectangle or another shape that covers the remaining visible footprint.</p>
<h2 id="batch">3. Reuse the text mask when placement repeats</h2><p>For same-ratio images with the same visible wordmark in the same relative position, angle, and size, One mask for all can apply the text mask across the batch. If layouts, rotation, or watermark placement differ, use Edit individually and adjust the exceptions.</p>
<h2 id="limitations">4. Review letter edges after repair</h2><p>Text often sits on detailed backgrounds, so inspect the After preview carefully. Small text over smooth or repeating texture is usually easier than large letters crossing faces, sharp edges, or other unique detail. The tool edits visible selected pixels only and does not remove invisible provenance or metadata systems.</p>
</ArticleFrame>
</SiteLayout>
